Hi Emmanuel,

How are you installing SpaDES -- using install.packages()? Which Linux OS are you using? There are a number of system dependencies (not used by SpaDES directly but from some of its dependencies) that you likely don't have installed (take a look at the full output of the installation process -- it will tell you which dependency packages failed).

On Debian/Ubuntu you likely need:

sudo apt install \
fftw3 fftw3-dev \
gdal-bin \
libcairo2-dev \
libgdal-dev libgdal1-dev \
libgeos-dev libgeos++-dev \
libproj-dev \
libspatialite-dev \
libudunits2-dev \
libv8-dev \
libxt-dev \
python-gdal python3-gdal

From u98614b368d11c847 at groups.google.com  Sun Apr 22 03:21:50 2018
From: u98614b368d11c847 at groups.google.com (Eliot McIntire)
Date: Sun, 22 Apr 2018 03:21:50 +0000
Subject: prepInputs and downloadData -- now moved to SpaDES.tools
Message-ID: <QwSqk8cc_QI.EnbsvaJbAQAJ@spades-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/spades-users/c/QwSqk8cc_QI/m/EnbsvaJbAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

prepInputs and downloadData and several of their helper/internal functions have been moved out of SpaDES.core and into SpaDES.tools.

This may or may not affect people. If a project was already loading SpaDES.tools (if the project was using spread, spread2, randomPolygon etc.), then it will have no effect. The solution to "couldn't find function prepInputs" is: library(SpaDES.tools)

This also caused downloadData and a few helper functions to be moved to SpaDES.tools, which may affect situations where downloadData was run manually, but will not affect situations where downloadModule(..., data = TRUE) was run.

Apologies for the inconvenience; it is the more natural place for these two functions. It is not really easy to officially deprecate the function because it still retains the same name. Similarly, adding SpaDES.tools to depends did not seem like the best solution (though this is open for comment).

